Creating a Simulation Model
You create your simulation model in several steps.
• To create a new Plant Simulation model, click Create New Model on the Start Page or select File >
New.
• To show the built-in simulation objects and their graphics, you can, for example, click the folder
MaterialFlow in the Class Library with the right mouse button and select Open in 3D. This way you
can also show the contents of the other folders.
To show the name of an object, drag the mouse over it.
To rotate the scene, hold down the left and the right mouse buttons and drag the mouse or hold down
Ctrl and the right mouse button and drag the mouse. The rotate mouse pointer looks like this 
 .
To pan the scene, hold down the right mouse button and drag the mouse. The pan mouse pointer looks
like this 
 .
To move the camera forward and backward, roll the mouse wheel.
To zoom the scene in or out, roll the mouse wheel or hold down Shift and the right mouse button and
drag the mouse.
Note
If you have a three-button mouse, click the middle mouse button where the description calls for
clicking the mouse wheel.
Note
If manipulating the scene with the mouse does not work, check the Mouse Properties for the Wheel
in the Mouse Control Panel under Start > Control Panel.
• To open a new window, change to the Class Library. Then, click the corresponding folder with the right
mouse button and select Open in 3D.
• To insert an object into the window:
◦ Make sure that Show Grid 
  on the View ribbon tab is active, compare Show and Hide the Grid.
We recommend to show the grid before you insert an object into the scene window. This way you can
place the object at the exact position, where you want it to be.
If you insert an object without displaying the grid, you have little control over where Plant Simulation
actually places the object. By default, it uses the same coordinates that the source object had, an
effect you might not want.
◦ Select the object you want to insert in the Toolbox. Drag the mouse pointer to the position of your
choice and click the left mouse button to insert it there.
Creating a Simulation Model
Plant Simulation Help 10-891
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

As long as an object is selected in the Toolbox, Plant Simulation shows a preview of the object in the
window to make it easier for you to position it.
To cancel inserting objects, click the right mouse button.
◦ Insert a Source, a Conveyor, a Station and a Drain.
• Connect the inserted simulation objects:
◦ Make sure that Show Connections 
  on the View ribbon tab is activated as 3D does not show the
Connectors by default.
◦ Click the Connector 
  in the Toolbox on the tab MaterialFlow.
◦ Click the source object, where the connection will start, once with the left mouse button. Plant
Simulation attaches a line symbolizing the Connector to the mouse pointer.
◦ Drag the mouse to the destination object at which the connection will end, and click once with the
left mouse button to establish the connection.
3D shows the Connector it inserted as a line between the connected internal interfaces of the source
and destination objects. The pointed cone shows the direction of the Connector.
◦ To terminate connect mode before you click the destination object, click the right mouse button or
press Esc.
To connect several objects one after the other, without interrupting the connecting process, hold
down Ctrl.
◊ Attach the outgoing connection to the first object.
◊ Drag the mouse to the succeeding object.
◊ Click the left mouse button to attach the incoming Connector to the successor.
Creating a Simulation Model
10-892 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◊ Connect the Source, the Conveyor, the Station and the Drain you inserted above.
◦ Start the simulation:
◊ Control the simulation by clicking the buttons on the Home ribbon tab or
◊ Double-click the icon of the EventController 
  and click its buttons.
◊ Watch how the produced parts move across the simulation objects.
