Modeling Hierarchically [in 3D]
Using hierarchical modeling techniques you can add any level of detail to the areas and machines you
insert into your simulation model. Hierarchical modeling means that you create a machine, a production
area, etc. in a Frame using the built-in object classes and/or classes you yourself designed.
You can then insert this Frame, Machine for example, into another Frame in which you modeled a
production area, and then insert the production area into the Frame that contains your entire factory.
Compare Model Hierarchically [general]
Compare Show Reports in a Hierarchically Structured Model
Back to Visualizing the Material Flow
Working with the Scene
Work with the Scene
Plant Simulation provides a number of ways for working with the active scene, as opposed to working with
the objects you insert into that scene.
You can:
• Manipulate the Scene with the Mouse
• Align the View to the Main Directions
• Save a View with the Model and Return to It
• Fly on a Defined Path Through the Scene
• Show information about the open scene by pressing the F key on the keyboard. To hide the information,
press the F key again. The scene window shows:
• The Frames per Second (FPS).
• The summed up Number of Nodes of the scene window.
• The summed up Number of Polygons of the scene window.
• The summed up Memory usage of the scene window.
• The OpenGL Version used.
• Information about the Display Driver of your computer.
Modeling Hierarchically [in 3D]
