Flying on a Defined Path Through the Scene
Fly on a Defined Path Through the Scene
You can attach a camera to an object and view the scene through the lens of an object. This camera then
moves along with the object through the scene during a simulation run. By turning or tilting the camera,
you can control the direction and the angle at which you look at the scene.
3D visualizes the camera with a tool object as the icon of the camera. At present 3D provides three
different cameras:
• The main camera through which you view the scene in normal view.
• The object camera, which you attach to a specific object and which moves with this object through the
model. You might, for example, attach a camera to a part or a Worker to follow it or him on their way
through the factory.
• The animation camera, which moves along an animation path you defined.
Proceed as follows to define a tracking shot through the scene:
• Click an object with the left mouse button to select it.
• Click 
  Animate Camera on the View ribbon tab to open the dialog Fly on Path. If you did not select
an object before, the dialog refers to the current root object of the scene.
• Select one of the camera paths you defined to move your view point along this path.
You can also define your own camera paths or edit any existing path in the dialog Edit 3D Properties >
Tab Camera Animation of the Frame to meet you specific modeling needs. Compare How the Camera is
Animated in the Frame.
You can:
• Attach a Camera to an Object
• Detach the Camera from an Object
• Animate the Object Camera
Compare Ballhaus Kreisel
Flying on a Defined Path Through the Scene
Plant Simulation Help 10-903
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Compare Work with Animation Paths
Compare Tab Camera Animation > How the Camera is Animated in the Frame
Back to Work with the Scene
Attach a Camera to an Object
You can attach a camera to an object, to view the scene through the lens of this camera.
Proceed as follows:
• Select a single object.
• Click 
  Attach Camera on the View ribbon tab of the 3D window.
• Plant Simulation attaches the camera at a defined position:
Plant Simulation uses the center of the top of the bounding box of the selected object as the position
where it inserts the camera. Plant Simulation adds an offset of 0,0,0.1 to these makeshift points, so that
your eyes will be located above the top plane.
By default the object camera looks along the positive x-axis using the positive z-axis as Up direction.
You might, for example, attach a camera to one of the boxes in our sample model Factory51 and track
its way through the factory.
Attach a Camera to an Object
10-904 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

As soon as the object, with a camera attached to it, moves through the scene, the camera moves along
with it automatically. Once the camera is attached, you see the scene through the lens of this object
camera.
Attach a Camera to an Object
Plant Simulation Help 10-905
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Or you can attach a camera to one of the Workers and track his way between the work stations.
Attach a Camera to an Object
10-906 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

You then see what the Worker sees while he walks and what he sees while he stands on the Workplaces
and works at the machines.
